| Description = Built in 1885-1891, this Richardsonian Romanesque Revival-style monument was designed by Louis Rebisso to honor United States Army General and former United States President Ulysses S. Grant (1822-1885).  The monument features a rough-hewn gray granite base with roman arched openings, which once carried a carriage path across a berm while allowing for unimpeded travel beneath, a gray rough-hewn granite pavilion with roman arched openings, and a bronze statue of Grant on horseback atop the roof of the pavilion.  The monument is one of several in Lincoln Park, and is characteristic of large-scale late 19th Century monument design, which often incorporated some form of functionality into their design.
